Subject: network: QDnsLookup without threads, over a wasi libresolv

WHAT / WHY

  QDnsLookup is the only API for the DNS record types getaddrinfo cannot
  express -- MX, SRV, TXT, NS, PTR, CNAME, SOA. On this port it was doubly
  unreachable, and both reasons had to go.

  src/network/configure.cmake
      `qt_feature("dnslookup" ... CONDITION QT_FEATURE_thread AND NOT
      INTEGRITY)`. FEATURE_thread is OFF here and is not coming back (wasip2
      components have no threads), so the whole feature compiled out. The
      thread condition is dropped; NOT INTEGRITY stays.

  src/network/kernel/qdnslookup.cpp
      The reason for that condition: QDnsLookupThreadPool derives from
      QThreadPool, and qthreadpool.cpp lives inside
      `qt_internal_extend_target(Core CONDITION QT_FEATURE_thread)` -- with
      threads off the class does not exist at all, so the file cannot even
      link. Both things the pool did are done inline instead:

        * DELIVERY. The pool version connects finished() with
          BlockingQueuedConnection, which on one thread is a deadlock that Qt
          asserts on. DirectConnection is not an approximation of it: it is
          what BlockingQueuedConnection degenerates to when sender and
          receiver share a thread, i.e. run() returns only after the slot has
          run, which is the ordering the threaded build guarantees.
        * OWNERSHIP. QRunnable autoDelete means the pool deletes the runnable.
          abort() only clears QDnsLookupPrivate's pointer without deleting,
          and ~QDnsLookup() is empty, so deleting it after run() is both
          required and safe; a late abort is already handled by the `d->runnable
          == sender()` check in the finished lambda, which drops the result.

      The invocation is QUEUED, not direct, so lookup() keeps its asynchronous
      contract -- finished() must not be emitted before lookup() returns, or a
      caller that connects afterwards (which the class documentation's own
      example does) would miss it. The query itself then blocks the event loop
      for its duration; that is unavoidable without a thread and is bounded by
      the resolver timeout.

  The other half is not a patch: wasi-libc ships <arpa/nameser.h> but no
  resolver, so upstream would fall back to qdnslookup_dummy.cpp, which errors
  on every lookup. plugins/resolv-compat supplies res_ninit/res_nmkquery/
  res_nsend/dn_expand (plus the legacy global _res that qhostinfo_unix.cpp
  wants) over ordinary sockets, which is all Qt borrows -- qdnslookup_unix.cpp
  parses every record type itself. With FEATURE_libresolv=ON that upstream
  file builds unmodified.

UPSTREAMABLE? The configure.cmake half, plausibly: nothing about
qdnslookup.cpp's logic needs a thread, and Qt already supports
-no-feature-thread. The single-threaded delivery path would need to be
written the way Qt writes them (a QT_CONFIG(thread) arm, as here) and to
survive their CI on a platform nobody currently builds that way.

--- a/src/network/configure.cmake
+++ b/src/network/configure.cmake
@@ -314,7 +314,7 @@
     SECTION "Networking"
     LABEL "QDnsLookup"
     PURPOSE "Provides API for DNS lookups."
-    CONDITION QT_FEATURE_thread AND NOT INTEGRITY
+    CONDITION NOT INTEGRITY
 )
 qt_feature("gssapi" PUBLIC
     SECTION "Networking"
--- a/src/network/kernel/qdnslookup.cpp
+++ b/src/network/kernel/qdnslookup.cpp
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 
 static Q_LOGGING_CATEGORY(lcDnsLookup, "qt.network.dnslookup", QtCriticalMsg)
 
+#if QT_CONFIG(thread)
 namespace {
 struct QDnsLookupThreadPool : QThreadPool
 {
@@ -38,6 +39,7 @@
 }
 
 Q_APPLICATION_STATIC(QDnsLookupThreadPool, theDnsLookupThreadPool);
+#endif // QT_CONFIG(thread)
 
 static bool qt_qdnsmailexchangerecord_less_than(const QDnsMailExchangeRecord &r1, const QDnsMailExchangeRecord &r2)
 {
@@ -799,9 +801,41 @@
     };
 
     d->runnable = new QDnsLookupRunnable(d);
+#if QT_CONFIG(thread)
     connect(d->runnable, &QDnsLookupRunnable::finished, this, l,
             Qt::BlockingQueuedConnection);
     theDnsLookupThreadPool->start(d->runnable);
+#else
+    // Without threads there is no pool to hand this to, and the two things the
+    // pool did have to be done here instead: deliver the result, and own the
+    // runnable.
+    //
+    // DirectConnection, not BlockingQueued: a blocking-queued connection whose
+    // sender and receiver are the same thread is a deadlock, and Qt asserts on
+    // it. Direct delivery is not an approximation of the threaded behaviour --
+    // it is what BlockingQueuedConnection degenerates to once there is only one
+    // thread, i.e. run() returns only after `l` has run, which is exactly the
+    // ordering the pool version guarantees.
+    connect(d->runnable, &QDnsLookupRunnable::finished, this, l, Qt::DirectConnection);
+    // Queued, so lookup() keeps its asynchronous contract: finished() must not
+    // be emitted before lookup() returns, or a caller that connects after
+    // calling lookup() -- legal, and what the docs' own example does -- would
+    // miss it. The query itself still blocks the event loop for its duration;
+    // that is unavoidable without a thread, and it is bounded by the resolver
+    // timeout (res_ninit's retrans*retry, 10s by default).
+    //
+    // The runnable is the context object, so this is cancelled if it dies. It
+    // cannot die behind our back: abort() only clears QDnsLookupPrivate's
+    // pointer without deleting (in the threaded build the pool owns the
+    // deletion), ~QDnsLookup() is empty, and the runnable is unparented -- so
+    // deleting it here is both required and safe. An abort that lands first is
+    // handled by `l`'s own `d->runnable == sender()` check, which drops the
+    // late result.
+    QMetaObject::invokeMethod(d->runnable, [r = d->runnable] {
+        r->run();
+        delete r;
+    }, Qt::QueuedConnection);
+#endif
 }
 
